notes[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-26T14:58:07.558Z","harness:claude-code","Root cause: findSourceRepositoryRoot walked up to any .git marker and ignored GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES. Fix follows Git's own discovery rule, checked against git rev-parse --show-toplevel for nine scenarios: the start directory is always examined, the walk never moves up into a ceiling, only absolute entries count (path.delimiter separated), entries are resolved to physical paths up to the first empty entry and kept as spelled after it, and Git compares them with the physical directories it walks, so an unresolved entry spelled through a symbolic link never matches. Returned coordinates keep their existing logical spelling. Covered by tests/unit/extensions/portable-workspace-ceiling.spec.ts (real directories and a link or junction) and an end-to-end case in extension-host-contracts.integration.spec.ts that runs an installed extension command with Git on PATH and with a Git-free PATH."
  "2026-11-26T15:59:08.399Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 on PR #1789, both P2 findings confirmed against git rev-parse and fixed. (1) A link start directory: Git walks the physical ancestors, so with root/wslink -> parent/ws/child2 and ceiling parent/ws Git finds no repository, while the logical walk reached parent. Discovery now walks physical ancestors and maps the result back to the caller's spelling where a logical ancestor resolves to it, else reports the physical path. (2) Ignored entries became active: before the first empty entry an entry Git cannot resolve (parent/missing/..) is dropped, and after it an entry is compared as spelled with one trailing separator trimmed, so child/.. is inert. Seven unit cases now, three of which fail on the previous implementation."
  "2026-11-26T16:52:10.814Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 on PR #1789: three discovery findings confirmed with git and fixed. A start spelled through a link and then dot-dot now resolves as spelled before normalization, as chdir does; one discovery now decides both coordinates, with no rediscovery from a normalized fallback; pm_root_rel is computed between physical paths when both sides resolve, so a source reached through a link alias keeps its tracker-relative path. Only the platform's own separators are trimmed after an empty ceiling entry, so a trailing backslash on POSIX names another directory as Git reads it. Round 2 adds four unit cases; three fail on the previous implementation."
  "2026-11-26T17:05:59.839Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 3 on PR #1789: without a checkout, the source root was the normalized spelling of the start (link/.. collapsed lexically), not the directory the walk started from, so a tracker could be classified as contained in a directory discovery never examined. discoverSourceWorkspace now returns the source and the checkout from one walk; without a checkout the source is the physical start unless the caller's spelling names the same directory."
  "2026-11-26T17:16:20.718Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 on PR #1789, recorded residual: before the first empty entry Git resolves a ceiling component by component and accepts a regular-file component followed by .. (README.md/.. names its directory), while realpathSync.native rejects it with ENOTDIR, so such an entry is dropped instead of becoming a ceiling. Replicating Git's resolver for a ceiling spelled through a file is out of proportion; a ceiling that names a directory behaves as Git's. CodeRabbit: an entry after an empty one is now compared in the walk's native separator form (Git for Windows writes C:/parent)."
files[2]{path,scope,note}:
